{
  "description": "Futures account history paging (GET /api/v1/futures/fills and /api/v1/futures/funding), for an SDK's iterate-all helper. Rules: (1) the cursor is opaque text, sent back exactly as received (percent-encoded per RFC 3986 in the query; the server decodes it to the same text); (2) a page may be short, even empty, and still carry a next_cursor: keep paging until next_cursor is null; (3) a BUSY answer means the provider cannot serve the page right now. It is either an EMPTY page whose next_cursor equals the cursor just sent, or an HTTP 503 whose body has details.reason \"futures_data_unavailable\" (on any request, including the first one, which sends no cursor). On a busy answer: wait, then request the same cursor again (no cursor stays no cursor), at most max_busy_retries times (default 3) in a row; both kinds of busy answer share this one counter, and a page that advances resets it. The wait is the server hint, taken in this order: the Retry-After header (delta-seconds or HTTP-date, as the SDK's transport parses it), then details.retry_after_seconds; a hint that is missing, unparseable, negative or zero is skipped (it never fails the call or busy-loops), and with no usable hint the wait is the SDK's normal backoff. A hinted wait is exact (no jitter); a hint above the SDK's maximum server wait fails at once, as for any request. This budget is a setting of its own, independent of the client's request retry count: the request-retry layer does NOT retry a futures_data_unavailable 503 inside the helper, so a client with retries disabled still rides out a busy provider and the attempts per cursor are at most 1 + max_busy_retries. With max_busy_retries 0 the first busy answer fails at once. When the budget runs out, fail with a retryable error (code PAGING_STALLED, a local error) without returning a partial result as complete; when the last busy answer was a 503, that error is attached as the cause, so its details.reason stays reachable. (4) a NON-empty page whose next_cursor equals the cursor just sent is a server error: yield its rows, then fail with a local error code PAGING_CURSOR_REPEATED, not retryable (never loop); (5) has_account false ends iteration with no rows; (6) the first request sends no cursor. Each case lists the pages in order: `request_cursor` is what the SDK must send (null = parameter absent), `response` what the server answers. `expect.ids` are the yielded row ids in order. (7) any OTHER retryable error mid-paging (e.g. a 503 without details.reason futures_data_unavailable, a 502, a 429) is retried with the client's normal request-retry policy for the SAME cursor and paging continues; a client with retries disabled fails at once; a page entry with `http_status` is an error answer whose body is `response` and whose headers are `headers`. Optional case fields: `client.max_retries` is the client's request retry count (absent = SDK default); `max_busy_retries` overrides the helper's budget for that case (absent = the top-level default). Optional expect fields: `sleep_seconds` lists the exact hinted waits in order; `backoff_sleeps` counts waits that must come from the SDK's normal backoff (no usable hint); `error_cause_reason` is the details.reason of the error attached as the cause of the final error. Real cursors look like \"<ms>.<key>\" or a bare \"<ms>\": still opaque. `funding_cases` repeat the rules on GET /api/v1/futures/funding, whose rows have no id: `expect.times` lists the yielded rows' `time`.",
